bnets, or Multi-AZ unless     \u2502\n\u2502  explicitly requested.                                                       \u2502\n\u2502  1. Identify necessary providers and resources.                              \u2502\n\u2502  2. You MUST design the project with a modular layout (Root calling          \u2502\n\u2502  submodules under `modules/` like `modules/networking/`,                     \u2502\n\u2502  `modules/storage/`, etc.).                                                  \u2502\n\u2502  3. You MUST provide a **Mermaid.js diagram string** representing the CLOUD  \u2502\n\u2502  INFRASTRUCTURE TOPOLOGY & DATA FLOW:                                        \u2502\n\u2502     - Visualize actual cloud resources, components, and traffic/data flows   \u2502\n\u2502  (e.g. `Client / App --> S3 Bucket --> Glacier Archive (30 Days)` or         \u2502\n\u2502  `Internet --> ALB --> EKS Nodes --> RDS`).                                  \u2502\n\u2502     - Include cloud service names and relationship labels.                   \u2502\n\u2502     - NEVER diagram Terraform file names (`main.tf`, `variables.tf`,         \u2502\n\u2502  `outputs.tf`) or directory trees in the Mermaid diagram.                    \u2502\n\u2502  4. The first line of your response must be: PROJECT_SLUG: <slug>            \u2502\n\u2502     - The slug MUST be a descriptive kebab-case name derived from the        \u2502\n\u2502  requirement.                                                                \u2502\n\u2502     - NEVER use generic names like \"terraform-project\" or timestamps.        \u2502\n\u2502                                                                              \u2502\n\u2502  EKS-SPECIFIC RULES:                                                         \u2502\n\u2502  - If creating EKS, ALWAYS include at least 2 public subnets in 2 different  \u2502\n\u2502  AZs (EKS requires this).                                                    \u2502\n\u2502  - ALWAYS include IAM